{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And in the hand of the priest there shall be the water of bitterness that causes the curse</b> – The commentators said that Scripture calls them by the name of their end result, that bitterness will occur in them, for HaShem will make her bitter greatly and they will curse her. And our Sages said likewise in the Sifrei (Naso 57): They are called מרים on account of their end result, for they embitter the body and invalidate the sin. But in the Gemara (Sotah 20a) they said that the priest puts something bitter in the water, and this is to arouse her. And if so, when it says \"and the water that causes the curse shall enter into her for bitterness\" (Numbers 5:24), it would have been proper for it to say 'and the bitter water that causes the curse shall enter into her.' And in the Sifrei (Naso 68) further: \"And he shall wipe into the water of bitterness\" (Numbers 5:23) — this teaches that the writing makes the water bitter. And the correct interpretation in my eyes is according to its plain sense, that when the woman drinks the water they will be sweet to her like other water, and afterwards when they enter her body, if she has defiled herself, they will arouse her and she will immediately feel bitterness in her mouth and in her body. And this is what it says (Numbers 5:27): \"And he shall make her drink the water, and it shall come to pass, if she is defiled and has acted treacherously against her husband, that the water that causes the curse shall enter into her for bitterness\" — for after the drinking, when they enter her belly, immediately they will turn bitter in her mouth and in her body, in accordance with the nature of emetic and purgative substances, which make those who drink them feel great bitterness. And afterwards her belly shall swell and her thigh shall fall. And they are called <b>מאררים</b> (that which curses) on account of the curses erased into them, with which they curse her. And the commentators said that the word <b>מי</b> is like 'water of,' with the construct coming in place of the genitive, just as its opposite occurs: \"מַיִם בִּרְכַּיִם\" (Ezekiel 47:4). And R. Abraham ibn Ezra said (on Numbers 5:18) that <b>מי</b> is construct, and the word <b>המרים</b> is an epithet of the noun, and if so, its secret is known. And this is not correct in my eyes, because it says \"and the water that causes the curse shall enter into her for bitterness.\""], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "Ramban here appears to critique Ibn Ezra's grammatical analysis of מי המרים as construct + epithet, but his closing objection targets the formulation of Numbers 5:24, which seems to support rather than undermine reading מרים as an adjective. The logical connection is not fully transparent in the Hebrew."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:03:04.412811873Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}